This was based on the 3.15 PR: https://github.com/dotnet/corefx/pull/43129/files

Fixes issue dotnet/runtime#57472

Main pr dotnet/runtime#57479

Also backported to 5.0 in dotnet/runtime#57562 and 3.1 in #43102. This PR will pave the way for backporting it to the `System.Memory` OOB package for .NET Framework.

# Customer Impact

A customer has encountered dotnet/runtime#57472 while on .NET Framework and reported it in dotnet/runtime#67295, necessiating backporting it once again. As an example provided in that issue, this memory manager:

``` csharp
private sealed class BasicMemoryManager : MemoryManager<byte>
{
    private readonly byte[] _buffer;
    public BasicMemoryManager(int size = 1024) => _buffer = new byte[size];

    protected override bool TryGetArray(out ArraySegment<byte> segment)
    {
        segment = new ArraySegment<byte>(_buffer);
        return true;
    }
    public override Span<byte> GetSpan() => _buffer;

    public override MemoryHandle Pin(int elementIndex = 0) => throw new NotSupportedException();
    public override void Unpin() => throw new NotSupportedException();
    protected override void Dispose(bool disposing) { }
}
```

will fail two of the following tests:

``` csharp
[Theory]
[InlineData(0, 32)]
[InlineData(0, 29)]
[InlineData(32, 32)] // fail: length 0
[InlineData(32, 29)] // fail: length -3
public void OffsetTest(int offset, int length)
{
    var mgr = new BasicMemoryManager(1024);
    var slice = mgr.Memory.Slice(offset, length);
    Assert.Equal(length, slice.Length);

    var ros = new ReadOnlySequence<byte>(slice);
    Assert.True(ros.IsSingleSegment);
    Assert.Equal(length, ros.Length);
}
```

# Testing

Failing test has been added in this PR.

# Risk

Low, as the change is very simple and obvious when you look at `ReadOnlySequence<T>` ctor that accepts `ReadOnlyMemory<T>` (notice line 133 versus lines 140 and 148):

https://github.com/dotnet/corefx/blob/0d40b482e6b6c232131dc808182d6b32d9d37394/src/System.Memory/src/System/Buffers/ReadOnlySequence.cs#L130-L149

Moreover, the changed code gets triggered only when creating a `ReadOnlySequence` from a `ReadOnlyMemory` backed by a `MemoryManager`. All other use cases (including creating from an array or from a `ReadOnlyMemory` backed by an array or a string) are unaffected. And the changes are identical to those in the other backports.

# Regression

This is not a regression.

**Version Used**:

- .NET SDK 11.0.100-rc.2.26504.105 (`dotnet format whitespace`)
- Roslyn `main` at [104dbc4](https://github.com/dotnet/roslyn/commit/104dbc475173ec2386f7b902336090cb04a60021) (formatting unit test)

**Steps to Reproduce**:

1. Save the following as `Program.cs` in an empty folder:

   ```csharp
   class C
   {
       void First()
       {
           int[] a = [1, 2];
       }

       void Second(object o)
       {
           switch (o)
           {
               case int i:
                   break;
           }
       }

       string M(string[] items)
       {
           if (items.Length > 0
               && items is
               [
                   {
                       Length: 1
                   }
               ])
           {
               return "";
           }

           return null;
       }

       void Third(object o)
       {
           switch (o)
           {
               case int i:
                   break;
           }
       }
   }
   ```

2. Run `dotnet format whitespace <folder> --folder --verify-no-changes`.
3. In `First()`, replace `int[] a = [1, 2];` with `int a = 1;`, and run the command again.

Reduced from a project file where changing a collection expression in one method toggled IDE0055 on a list pattern in another.

**Diagnostic Id**:

`IDE0055: Fix formatting` (reported as `WHITESPACE` by `dotnet format whitespace`).

**Expected Behavior**:

The file is already formatted, so step 2 reports no changes, the same as step 3. How the list pattern in `M` is formatted does not depend on the body of `First()`.

**Actual Behavior**:

Step 2 exits with code 2 and reports three changes, all inside the list pattern:

```text
Program.cs(22,13): error WHITESPACE: Fix whitespace formatting. Delete 4 characters.
Program.cs(23,17): error WHITESPACE: Fix whitespace formatting. Delete 4 characters.
Program.cs(24,13): error WHITESPACE: Fix whitespace formatting. Delete 4 characters.
```

Applying them moves the property pattern to the column of `[`:

```diff
         if (items.Length > 0
             && items is
             [
-                {
-                    Length: 1
-                }
+            {
+                Length: 1
+            }
             ])
```

Step 3 exits with code 0. Its edit is in an unrelated method. Deleting `First()`, `Second`, or `Third` instead also makes the file format without changes.

**Analysis**:

For a list pattern whose only element is a property pattern, `IndentBlockFormattingRule` emits two indent block operations with the same text span, from the `{` to the `}` of the property pattern:

- the list pattern's content indentation, one level in from the enclosing indentation ([IndentBlockFormattingRule.cs#L264](https://github.com/dotnet/roslyn/blob/104dbc475173ec2386f7b902336090cb04a60021/src/Workspaces/SharedUtilitiesAndExtensions/Compiler/CSharp/Formatting/Rules/IndentBlockFormattingRule.cs#L264))
- the property pattern clause's alignment to the line of `[`, with a delta of 0 ([IndentBlockFormattingRule.cs#L193](https://github.com/dotnet/roslyn/blob/104dbc475173ec2386f7b902336090cb04a60021/src/Workspaces/SharedUtilitiesAndExtensions/Compiler/CSharp/Formatting/Rules/IndentBlockFormattingRule.cs#L193))

`FormattingContext.AddIndentBlockOperation` keeps the first operation it receives for a span and ignores later ones ([FormattingContext.cs#L184](https://github.com/dotnet/roslyn/blob/104dbc475173ec2386f7b902336090cb04a60021/src/Workspaces/SharedUtilitiesAndExtensions/Compiler/Core/Formatting/Context/FormattingContext.cs#L184)). The order they arrive in comes from `Sort(CommonFormattingHelpers.IndentBlockOperationComparer)` ([FormattingContext.cs#L130](https://github.com/dotnet/roslyn/blob/104dbc475173ec2386f7b902336090cb04a60021/src/Workspaces/SharedUtilitiesAndExtensions/Compiler/Core/Formatting/Context/FormattingContext.cs#L130), and [#L170](https://github.com/dotnet/roslyn/blob/104dbc475173ec2386f7b902336090cb04a60021/src/Workspaces/SharedUtilitiesAndExtensions/Compiler/Core/Formatting/Context/FormattingContext.cs#L170) for a partial span). The comparer returns `0` for two operations with the same span unless both are relative ([CommonFormattingHelpers.cs#L22-L43](https://github.com/dotnet/roslyn/blob/104dbc475173ec2386f7b902336090cb04a60021/src/Workspaces/SharedUtilitiesAndExtensions/Compiler/Core/Utilities/CommonFormattingHelpers.cs#L22-L43)), and the sort is an introsort ([ArraySortHelper.cs#L145](https://github.com/dotnet/roslyn/blob/104dbc475173ec2386f7b902336090cb04a60021/src/Dependencies/Collections/Internal/ArraySortHelper.cs#L145)), which does not keep equal elements in their original order. Which of the two operations wins is therefore decided by every other operation in the document.

Tracing `AddIndentBlockOperation` shows the alignment arriving first in the repro, so `{` gets the column of `[`. In the step 3 variant the content indentation arrives first, so `{` gets the column of the `&&` line plus one indentation level.

As an experiment, breaking ties by collection order at both sort calls makes the repro and the step 3 variant format the same way, and the existing tests in `Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.CSharp.Workspaces.UnitTests` still pass. The existing tests also produce the same tie for a list pattern whose only element is another list pattern, such as `[[]]`; whether that pair can also produce a visible difference was not investigated.

**Relationship to #73251**:

Issue #73251 reports the same symptom, the property pattern placed level with `[`, in a file with nothing else in it. There, `[` is indented further than the first token of the line that holds `is`, so both operations above compute the wrong column, and a stable sort leaves that case unchanged. This report is about which of the two operations applies. That only matters when `[` is level with the first token of that line, as with the `&&` continuation line in `M`, where the content indentation gives the right column and the alignment does not. A fix for #73251 that stops emitting the conflicting alignment would hide this instance, but any other pair of operations with the same span would still be resolved by sort order.

